{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"fb634608fc3e7b5d1d21e2414aa3cee61641bbf8b670a063cbfed518300ea01d","slug":"index-9","location":"/chapters/ch09_content_analysis/0_index.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"9 - Content Analysis & Metadata","authors":[{"id":"Roger Weber","name":"Roger Weber"}],"content_includes_title":false,"numbering":{"title":{"offset":1}},"exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"0_index.md","url":"/mmir-unibasel-hs26/book/build/0_index-2a65ea67e7b9d179c16f3d7195eb4366.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The previous chapters built a complete toolkit for text. An inverted file finds every document that contains a query term, BM25 ranks them, embeddings match “heart disease” with “cardiology”, vector indexes search millions of embeddings in milliseconds, and a language model turns the top passages into an answer. Now point the same system at a photo archive, a music catalogue, or a video library. The query is still text, but the documents are pixels, audio samples, and video frames. Nothing in a JPEG file says “Taj Mahal”. Can we do for images, audio, and video what we did for text?","position":{"start":{"line":13,"column":1},"end":{"line":13,"column":1}},"key":"Oht9xBKIQX"}],"key":"rxXgpNTslY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The answer comes in steps. The obvious fix is to describe every item with keywords and reuse text search. It works, and curated catalogues such as IMDb live on it, but human annotation does not scale and metadata from the wild cannot be trusted. The lasting answer is to extract descriptions from the content itself: perceptual features that describe what humans see and hear, classifiers that turn those features into labels, and multimodal transformers that either write a description for the text index or place images and text in one shared vector space. Audio and video add time as a new dimension, and some tasks, such as naming a song from a noisy phone recording, need solutions of their own. The chapters that follow open the toolbox for each modality; this chapter draws the map.","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"Kgvum3E4N1"}],"key":"l1LDQNVvQ5"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"tip","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What you’ll learn","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"Yf5y9WALoq"}],"key":"iJtolnSW51"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain the semantic gap and why it is wider for images, audio, and video than for text.","position":{"start":{"line":18,"column":1},"end":{"line":18,"column":1}},"key":"emZQy6Vt4P"}],"key":"WMsPLxafmk"}],"key":"ouzIIuYUwz"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Describe the types and facets of metadata and standards such as EXIF, IPTC, Dublin Core, and ID3, and explain why manual annotation neither scales nor can always be trusted.","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column":1},"end":{"line":19,"column":1}},"key":"rwbrAZRM3f"}],"key":"kDTar5Omuf"}],"key":"MJtRPoQgsd"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Distinguish the levels of automated content analysis, from perceptual features through learned classifiers to multimodal transformers.","position":{"start":{"line":20,"column":1},"end":{"line":20,"column":1}},"key":"Q6grK8kVmq"}],"key":"x5QpzWi5I6"}],"key":"mtiwW4ZQGp"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Compare the two transformer paths: generating text for a keyword index versus embedding text and media in a shared vector space such as CLIP.","position":{"start":{"line":21,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"pYQJrXMVTD"}],"key":"qlAynU7ZhB"}],"key":"mdlRih7qu1"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Explain why time-based media need segmentation into retrieval units, and recognize when a task calls for a specialized solution such as audio fingerprinting.","position":{"start":{"line":22,"column":1},"end":{"line":22,"column":1}},"key":"RbpqXO3hDa"}],"key":"zDKwoZ2EyA"}],"key":"PwDho9Voiu"}],"key":"v0e3ip28ji"}],"key":"hRtAJvc5k2"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"seealso","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Prerequisites","position":{"start":{"line":25,"column":1},"end":{"line":25,"column":1}},"key":"ngM3Oj530s"}],"key":"y1aZbqQyLQ"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":29,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Text retrieval with keywords: ","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key":"Xe5iFZKQI5"},{"type":"link","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Classical Text Retrieval","position":{"start":{"line":26,"column":1},"end":{"line":26,"column":1}},"key